= Contradiction — Technical =

== Classical form ==
For formula φ:

<pre>
φ ∧ ¬φ
</pre>

is unsatisfiable in classical semantics.

== Explosion ==
Classical consequence validates:

<pre>
φ, ¬φ ⊢ ψ
</pre>

for arbitrary ψ.

This principle is often called ''ex contradictione quodlibet'' or explosion.

== Paraconsistency ==
Paraconsistent logics reject unrestricted explosion.

They permit some inconsistent theories to remain nontrivial:

<pre>
φ, ¬φ ⊬ ψ
</pre>

for at least some ψ.

This changes the relation between contradiction and inconsistency.

== Negation dependence ==
What counts as contradiction depends partly on the logic's negation operator and semantics.

Intuitionistic, paraconsistent, relevant, and many-valued systems can therefore differ from classical logic.

